#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221155 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "fe6b394f-a130-5746-a5c9-0fbbee020554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21424" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "04233a8a-069f-5649-b198-c6bbf5b96742" :question/math-academy-id "e-21531" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0.$ Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}.$ Show the steps to compute this transform." :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-7t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221162 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1e5e6e3a-306b-5e8c-a8ba-baa0d63a1813" :question/math-academy-id "e-21549" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a < 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/id #uuid "05394517-ee39-5b77-9f84-b439d907dd3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21590" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{2{t}^{2}}+L{5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20110f1c-1589-54c9-b18f-40613b2435d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302659} {:answer/id #uuid "217c0f94-592f-5399-80c8-1115516f337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302666} {:answer/id #uuid "38f96532-324d-56d2-941c-05f48d18b144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302810} {:answer/id #uuid "f38176af-3dcb-56c6-ac4b-2f8e0f6286d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303983} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1c7d59-ed86-50ea-94af-9530e5107958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f38176af-3dcb-56c6-ac4b-2f8e0f6286d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303983} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7bfb225-081e-5616-a873-01ee47525e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b93a8ba-b183-509b-a5e3-8df4ea93eca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-328557" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8a4d5f-9a6d-54d5-8678-